herolte: deep idle only for idles longer than the state's residency (0443)

min72 (heroidle=0xee, six cores): enter 27382 / resume 23915 (cpu1) in 7 minutes, i.e. 60-70 power cycles a second per core, UI at 3 fps, screen-off current 491 mA = the same as with one core. Two causes: the 2 ms virtual-timer override of 0439 (a test for min69, no longer needed since 0441: the timer wake works) ended every sleep after 2 ms, and every idle, however short, went down. Now: no timer override (XNU's own pop, armed in SetIdlePop, wakes the core); deep idle only if the core's next timer pop (cpu_data rtcPop) is at least 3 ms away (Samsung's min-residency: 0.75 ms boot cluster, 2 ms the others); the watchdog wakes a sleeper after 1 s (was 150 ms) and gives up after 20 s (was 3 s). heroidle only (D-0089).

--- a/osfmk/arm64/cpu.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/cpu.c
@@ -503,25 +503,22 @@
 		return false;
 	}
 	volatile uint32_t *pcfg = (volatile uint32_t *)herolte_idle_pmu_cfg[c];
+	/*
+	 * 0443: min72 (six cores): every core entered and left deep idle ~60-70 times a second (enter 27382 / resume 23915 in 7
+	 * minutes), the UI ran at 3 fps and the screen-off current was the same as with one core: the 2 ms timer of 0439 cut every
+	 * sleep short, and short idles are not worth a power cycle. Linux does not go into cpu-sleep unless the next event is farther
+	 * than the state's min-residency (exynos-powermode.c get_next_event_time_us; DTB: 750 us boot cluster, 2000 us the others).
+	 * Here: the core's own next timer pop (what XNU armed in SetIdlePop: rtcPop) must be at least 3 ms away; otherwise plain WFI.
+	 */
+	{
+		uint64_t now_t = mach_absolute_time(), pop = cdp->rtcPop;
+		if (pop > now_t && pop - now_t < 78000ULL) {      /* 3 ms at 26 MHz */
+			return false;
+		}
+	}
 	herolte_idle_enter[c]++;
 	herolte_idle_since[c] = mach_absolute_time();
 	CleanPoC_Dcache();
-	/*
-	 * 0439: min68 (cpu 7 only): the core entered, never came back, 15 wake-up SGIs did nothing. An SGI may not be a wake source
-	 * of a powered-down core; the architected virtual timer (PPI 27, the one XNU uses) is what Linux's idle wakes on. Armed for
-	 * 2 ms ahead (52 000 ticks at 26 MHz) before the call; the timer is re-synchronized if the call returns.
-	 */
-	/*
-	 * 0441: what Samsung's Linux does before the SMC and min68-min70 did not (exynos-powermode.c enter_c2 ->
-	 * exynos_cpu.power_down -> exynos-pmu.c exynos_cpu_down): the core's PMU CONFIGURATION register, local power bits 0xf, goes
-	 * to 0, so that the WFI the monitor executes powers the core down and an interrupt can bring it back. On a return from the
-	 * call (error or no power-down) it goes back to 0xf, as Linux's wakeup_from_c2(early_wakeup) does.
-	 */
-	*pcfg = *pcfg & ~0xfu;
-	__builtin_arm_dsb(DSB_SY);
-	__builtin_arm_wsr64("CNTV_CVAL_EL0", __builtin_arm_rsr64("CNTVCT_EL0") + 52000ULL);
-	__builtin_arm_wsr64("CNTV_CTL_EL0", 1ULL);
-	__builtin_arm_isb(ISB_SY);
 	__builtin_arm_dsb(DSB_SY);
 	register uint64_t x0 __asm__("x0") = 0xC4000001ULL;     /* CPU_SUSPEND (the DTB's cpu_suspend) */
 	register uint64_t x1 __asm__("x1") = 0x10000ULL;        /* power state: power down (cpu-sleep) */
